  <rdf:Description rdf:nodeID="commodity_cittern_cbed954a34a189e3a66213735411cd63c1272de4c100c20d6243b4c7222095aa97">
    <crm:P190_has_symbolic_content>European wire-strung plucked lutelike chordophones played with a plectrum, very popular in the 16th and 17th centuries, having a wedge-shaped body with a curved bottom and straight shoulders narrowing towards the neck, and a low flat bridge held in position only by the strings it supports. Their most characteristic feature is a long, fretted neck which is half cut away from behind the fingerboard on the bass side, making a channel along which the player's left thumb can slide to facilitate very rapid shifts to and from high positions. [Source: Getty AAT]</crm:P190_has_symbolic_content>
    <crm:P2_has_type rdf:resource="http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object"/>
  </rdf:Description>
